    "hypothesis": "New checkout tasks crash-loop on startup: each freshly started task logs 'WARN memory pressure rising', attempts to allocate a 900MiB in-memory cache, then dies with MemoryError. ECS keeps relaunching replacement tasks (task IDs PHONE_3 through PHONE_10, each ~30-70s apart) to satisfy desired count, causing repeated pending/failed launches and tripping the alarm — while the two original long-running tasks (PHONE_1, PHONE_2) stay healthy throughout. Root cause looks like a memory-hungry startup cache allocation that exceeds the task's memory limit (container memory already at ~59% before the new task even starts), likely from a bad task definition/image change not reflected in the (empty) RECENT DEPLOYS field.",
